think grew sort (ascending; strings lexicographic, ints numeric, via std.mem.sort), unique (first-seen distinct via values_equal), and upper (mirror of lower) — the pieces claim_preserve needs to emit sorted-unique tokens
think folding claim_preserve surfaced three more gaps and I closed each — a single-char-class lookbehind (?<!X) for the PATH class (zero-width, checks one prior byte; general lookaround stays out), list + concatenation to combine the six token classes (Rishi strings keep \n literal, so no newline-join trick), and the do_pipeline list cap raised by heap-allocating the result at the input length (a map over a big find no longer truncates at 1024)
think caught two extractor bugs against the reference — I dropped the \b word boundaries every class but PATH uses, and the FP interpolation nested a pattern literal inside a string (Rishi's lexer closed the outer string early); fixed the \b and switched the prefixes to + concatenation
obs the fold is validated 40/41 real files byte-identical to the elder perl extractor; the one diff is a single PROPER token on LEXICON — Python's \b is Unicode-aware (Bashō is one word), an ASCII engine sees the byte boundary — on a file the gate never runs, and the gate compares a file's before vs after with the same extractor, so consistency not Python-parity is what it needs
obs folded the sibling claim_preserve_modality too (modal-word counts via length (find …)), byte-identical to Python; the whole claim_preserve gate now runs GREEN and python-free on this pier for the first time — python3 was absent, so it had been silently red
